WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.640
In 2012, I told a story about the future.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.960
I don't like looking back at things
I've made in the past.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.880
But in the last couple of weeks,
I've some a few emails saying:

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.480
Tom, ten years ago,
you tried to predict 2022,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.040
or at least some of the technology
and online world of 2022.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.080
You should look back
and see if you were right.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.640
So I've come back to the same place
that I gave that talk.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.480
Back in summer 2012 this was a
warm, friendly temporary festival site

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.840
with a load of people on it

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.040
and now it's a cold, run-down,
deserted bit of scrubland

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.280
next to a motorway,
but it's the same place.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.000
I did originally try to film this
without having looked back at the talk,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
to get my actual genuine reaction,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.920
and it was a terrible video,
I'm bad at improvising

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.320
and I thought of a dozen more things
I wanted to say right afterwards,

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.160
so now I've had some time to think...
let's go back ten years,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.840
see what I got right,
see what I got wrong,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.880
and see if I can predict
ten years from now.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.480
- I've got a story for you.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.760
It's set in a future.
Not necessarily the future,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.280
I'm not saying this is what will happen.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.600
- I think that's the first time
I ever used that phrase.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.960
It's a get-out-of-jail-free card,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.920
a way to hedge my bets and say
I'm probably going to wrong.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.080
I use it a lot when I
write speculative fiction.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.720
- April 2022. Ten years' time.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.880
First person we're going to
meet there is Jason Stewart.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.120
He's the MP for Tooting and Streatham.
He is the Minister for Social Development.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.880
Considered kind of a hotshot
among his party

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.920
after winning his seat in
2020 General Election

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.480
in a very hard-fought contest.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.240
- All the political guesses there
were completely wrong, but then,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.400
when has anyone been even able
to predict politics?

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.720
Also, the faces in this talk
weren't originally blurred,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.360
I've used YouTube's tools to go back
and blur them in the original as well,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.640
because there's a big difference
between asking a friend

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.680
"hey, can I borrow your headshot for
this talk I'm just giving to a few people"

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.400
and "can I show that to... however many people
will watch this new video".

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.840
I've got a duty of care there.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.800
- This is the 2022 iPhone.
Looks about the same,

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.040
the laws of physics dictate you
still need a battery brick.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.520
- That's a hit. It's a very easy hit,

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.413
"the borders of phone screens
are going to get smaller"

00:01:49.413 --> 00:01:51.040
is not exactly a difficult
prediction to make.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.080
And by sheer luck, I used a
black background on the phone,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.160
so you can't see if there's
a notch or not at the top.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.600
One subtle thing I got
right there as well:

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.800
Apple did change their font.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.720
Back in 2012, they were using Helvetica,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.440
I picked the wrong one to change to,
they use a custom sans-serif,

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.080
but I did predict the UI was
going to change a bit.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.960
- But what has changed is&nbsp;
the mobile network speed.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.400
5G now blankets the country in
hundred-megabit internet access,

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.080
and the data caps are a thing of the past.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.680
- Again, a fairly easy hit.
4G was just being rolled out in 2012,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.040
so taking a guess at 5G
in ten years wasn't that hard.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.480
And it actually was
a bit pessimistic:

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.520
there are parts of the UK where you
can get gigabit access on your phone now.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.680
Data caps are still a thing on some contracts,

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.160
but as far as I know they're not something
most people have to think too much about.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.560
As you watch this, you've got
to remember that in 2012

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.160
we were almost exactly half way
through the transition to smartphones,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.400
only about 50% of people had moved
from calls-and-texts-only to

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.560
what we'd call a modern phone today.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.080
Suddenly, half the population was
carrying a camera all the time,

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.120
half the population could easily
cheat on pub quizzes.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.600
Things were changing,
and they were changing fast,

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.760
but we weren't in the smartphone era.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.000
Social changes hadn't swept in, not yet.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.840
2012 me is about to get to the
fundamental idea of the talk,

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.480
and it's based on one of the big ideas
that was going around

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:05.680
among technical people at the time:

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.240
lifelogging, the quantified self:

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.560
the plausible idea that we going
store all our memories online soon.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.600
It was mainstream enough that Black Mirror's
"Entire History of You" had aired

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.960
just a few months earlier,

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.440
and now everyone was getting a computer
with internet access in their pocket.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.800
2012 was before Google Glass,
before the first Narrative Clip,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.360
all of which came and went very quickly.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.960
It turns out that, so far, people just
don't seem to like the idea

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.200
of remembering everything, and storing
that data forever with corporations.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.560
This talk felt like a warning,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.240
but I didn't know -- no-one knew --
if it a warning that was going to be needed.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.320
- Last year, Apple reused a trademark
and introduced iLife.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.120
It's quite similar to Your History
on Microsoft Nokia phones,

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.120
or Droid Locker on Android.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.280
They're all descendents of the
accountability systems

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.400
that have been used for police officers
and care workers for years now.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.760
- So many misses in there!
Apple have since switched to flat design.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.400
All the Android services have been
rebranded as Google.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.080
And Microsoft Nokia phones!

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.320
And the idea of private-contractor
body-worn cameras,

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.960
and that all sorts of people
all over the public and private sectors

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.040
would wear them every day as
part of their work...

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.840
it just hasn't come true.
Completely wrong.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.800
= With iLife, the phone is always recording audio

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.080
and sending it and your&nbsp;
location to a cloud server.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.120
The descendents of Siri then&nbsp;
generate a transcript of it.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.640
If you're talking to someone else with iLife,
it'll give you a text chat log.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.400
It's not perfect, but it's close enough.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.040
- Big miss. Companies just haven't developed that.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.880
People, I think, don't want that?

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.640
But back then, Siri was a few months old;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.800
Amazon's Alexa would come out
a couple of years later.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.320
I'm sure any of those companies could
have developed that product,

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.240
it's entirely possible these days,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.840
it's just that we went a different way.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.840
- And if you have the new Apple headset,
it's got a couple of cameras embedded in it,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.320
which are always recording
video when they're connected.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.000
That's uploaded, analysed, and
stabilised by the same systems.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.320
The cable, incidentally,&nbsp;
still gets tangled in knots&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.360
every time you put it in your bag.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.160
- Another big miss. To be fair to earlier me,

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.160
I still think wireless headphones
are a terrible idea.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.000
I cannot understand why anyone uses them.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.480
They're expensive, they need recharging,
the battery degrades over time,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.960
they need a carrying case, they're easy to lose,
Bluetooth is still really dodgy ten years later.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.440
I still use wired headsets.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.000
Now, in hindsight, wired&nbsp;
camera-buds would probably&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.600
have drained too much battery too quickly,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.040
but I still think there's a path
that could have got us there by now.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.560
- Which brings us back to Jason Stewart.
Jason is a member of 'Cheshire Boys',

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.040
the site for old alumni
of his public school.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.680
It runs on Disco, a fairly
popular community framework,

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.720
that unfortunately has some
fairly major security flaws.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.240
- That's one of the biggest misses
in this whole talk. And it's subtle.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.160
It took me a few times watching this
to realise just how badly I'd got that wrong.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.280
A community of school alumni wouldn't be
a web site or forum these days,

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.200
it wouldn't be independently hosted.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.360
There are still some places like that,
yes, but depending on demographics

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.360
that would almost certainly be a Facebook group,&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.080
or a WhatsApp thread, or&nbsp;
a Slack or Discord server.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.560
It would be on some centralised service,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.360
not a private web server
with some off-the-shelf code.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.920
That whole business model,
that way of operating,

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.320
was dying at the time
and I had not noticed.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.000
I'm skipping some of the talk here:

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.240
basically, that Member of&nbsp;
Parliament's password got leaked.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.000
- His password is 'stewart9',

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.600
that's the same password he uses everywhere,

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.600
and someone goes "hey!&nbsp;
Let's log into his iCloud!".

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.320
- Why is any of that on desktop?

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:20.960
2012 was the start of the
transition to mobile-first

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.560
and I just didn't see it coming.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.040
All these screenshots should&nbsp;
be vertical, on a phone.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:28.720
I must have been imagining people coming home
and checking this on their home computer,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.920
instead of getting notifications
and using an app on their device.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.640
A couple of years later that would be obvious,&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.800
everything would start to&nbsp;
be designed mobile-first,

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.080
but in 2012, after I'd spent more than a decade

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.960
of writing web sites on and for desktop computers,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.720
I just missed that entirely.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:46.720
That transition was starting, just starting...

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:48.640
and I hadn't spotted it.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:52.000
- First thing they see is Apple's
handy 'Is This You?' feature.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.600
Jason Stewart can be seen in a few frames
of this walk-by in Shoreditch.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.280
He wasn't recording, but his phone had his
location and facial recognition did the rest,

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.160
and it didn't take too much effort
to find out what he was doing there.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:06.640
A few minutes later, this
poison pen letter arrives in

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.280
every single political editor
and blogger's inbox in the country.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.960
The Sun eventually runs with it first,

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.200
after all the blogs have
made it public knowledge.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.520
- Blogging! Blogs were still a big thing
in the public consciousness in 2012,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.280
and I did not see their decline coming.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.080
I didn't see them being
mostly-replaced by Twitter.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.600
Should I have known? Was it obvious?

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.960
It seems clear in hindsight that
the "blogosphere"'s days were numbered,

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.400
but I missed it.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.600
About one year after that talk,
Google would switch off Google Reader,

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.440
forever gaining a reputation as a company
that shuts down old products

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:36.960
instead of maintaining them,

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.440
and it was all downhill from there.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:40.960
He decides he's going to go on the offence.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.560
He gets up in Parliament and says that

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:46.800
'our private lives should be private,
and we must investigate these hackers'.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.520
And he convinces the Met to
investigate, which they do by

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.240
filing a request with Apple for
any lifelogs that mentioned Jason Stewart

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.640
in the hours and minutes before the leak.

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.920
And Apple agrees, because that argument
got settled years ago,

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:00.720
...after Blackpool.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:03.520
You can tell something about what a
culture is worried about,

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.600
or maybe what a particular author
is worried about,

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.600
by reading their speculative fiction.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.720
I just finished "The Brief History of the Dead"
by Kevin Brockmeier,

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.400
and there are references in there to
"terrorist warning beacons"

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.640
that everyone's got used to
and just ignores,

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:20.560
and as I read that I thought,
"oh, this is a post-9/11 book".

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.080
And it is, it's from 2006.
And this talk, in 2012,

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.600
still very much in the consciousness of people
working online that we were

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.520
one media-friendly terrorist attack away from
losing what was left of our privacy rights.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.040
- Your lifelog will get pulled
and searched through by the police.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:38.280
it's not like you've got
anything to hide, right?

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.800
- Maybe we still are. It's just that
as far as I can tell from what I read,

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.040
science fiction in the 2020s is more worried

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.880
about democratic backsliding,
misinformation, and climate apocalypse.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.600
So there's one question left.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:53.840
What about ten years from now?

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.280
What about 2032?

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.840
I'm not immersed in web development
and technology any more,

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:59.840
I don't code very often,
I work on video now.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.760
So what can I predict?
Well, here's the guess.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:05.920
Not the future, just a future.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.800
I think short-form video is going
to do to YouTube

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:10.160
what Twitter did to blogs.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.160
People will still be making&nbsp;
long-form video content,

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:14.400
it will still get linked to and watched...

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.240
but short-form is so much simpler
that there will be so much more of it.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.520
A tweet takes ten seconds to write,
requires no proofreading or punctuation,

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.920
and can be amplified to the world in minutes.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:27.760
A blog post takes minutes or hours to write,
needs spell-checking and formatting,

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.360
and takes time to be discovered.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.640
It's not a perfect analogy,
but I have a sinking feeling

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:36.800
that short-form video is going to win
just through sheer weight of numbers,

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.800
the ability for almost anyone to compete

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.160
on the same, easy, low-effort,
low-attention span playing field.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.160
But there's no money in it.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.600
There's no money in Twitter, just
the ability to promote other stuff.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.840
There is still some money in
long-form writing,

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:53.120
through commissions, or fan subscriptions
on Patreon or other platforms.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.760
In the same way, I don't think short-form video
is going to kill YouTube.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.800
After all, YouTube didn't kill television,
and television didn't kill radio.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.560
But there's a transition happening,
just as there was ten years ago.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.080
If you want a prediction for 2032,

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:09.680
it's that the boom times for this platform,
for YouTube, will be over.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.240
There's one problem with that, though.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.000
YouTube is centralised.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.200
Blogs couldn't disappear because everyone
hosted their own, in loads of different places,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.160
often for a monthly fee.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:22.640
But YouTube... well, we'll have to see if,
in ten years' time,

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:31.840
Google decide to shut down&nbsp;
this old product as well.

